mode: "100644"
text: "# Platform Mechanics & When Not to Thread\n\nThe mechanics differ between X and Threads, and — just as important — not every idea should be a\nthread at all.\n\n> Specs change; treat numbers as approximate and re-verify. The principles are durable.\n\n## X / Twitter\n\n- **Limit:** 280 characters per post (free); much longer with Premium, but short posts travel\n  further and read better in a thread. Keep posts punchy.\n- **Thread culture:** strong. Threads are a native, expected format; numbering (\"1/\", \"🧵\") is\n  common and helps orientation.\n- **Reshare-the-opener mechanic:** the key growth move — the close asks people to RT post 1, which\n  drives new readers to the top. X-native and powerful.\n- **Links:** fine, but many believe in-thread links dampen reach — a common tactic is to put the\n  link in a **reply** to the thread, or in the final post. Lead with value, link last.\n- **Hashtags:** minimal (0–2) or none; more reads as spam.\n\n## Threads (Meta)\n\n- **Limit:** ~500 characters per post — roomier than X.\n- **Culture:** more casual and conversational; less \"thread culture\" than X (single posts and\n  short chains are common). Threads still work, but lean more relaxed and less numbered.\n- **Tags:** one topic tag per post, not multi-hashtag.\n- **Links:** clickable and fine.\n- **Adapt, don't clone:** a strong X thread can move to Threads, but loosen the register, drop\n  heavy numbering, and consider whether a couple of conversational posts serve better than a long\n  formal thread.\n\n## When NOT to thread (this is half the skill)\n\nA thread is the right format only when the idea genuinely needs **multiple beats**. Force one and\nit underperforms a single good post. Don't thread when:\n\n- **The point fits one post.** A single sharp insight is stronger as one punchy post than padded\n  into five. One idea, no sub-parts → single post.\n- **You're padding to hit a number.** If posts 4–8 are filler to \"make it a thread,\" it shouldn't\n  be one.\n- **There's no real payoff.** A thread promises a destination; if there's no lesson, list, story,\n  or result at the end, sharpen the idea before threading.\n- **It's a hot take or a one-liner.** Those live and die as single posts; a thread dilutes them.\n\nSigns it *should* be a thread: a list, a step-by-step, a story with beats, an argument that needs\nbuilding, or a breakdown with parts. If you can name the beats, it's a thread. If you can't, it's\na post.\n\nWhen unsure, default to the **single strong post** and offer the thread as an option — restraint\nreads as confidence and respects the reader's time.\n"
